esired, label this explicitly as a recycling route rather than de novo glycine synthesis.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P8\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_008\": 0.2\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.2,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.35,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.88,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.4,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.43,\n      \"feedback\": \"As encoded, this branch is not viable. rxn_008 is unbalanced and the checker reports that no simple common small species repairs it. The literature supports glycine as a major threonine decomposition product, but not a mass-conserving threonine -> glycine transformation with no coproducts.\",\n      \"approved\": false,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_008\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Replace rxn_008 with an explicit balanced degradation reaction that includes the missing C2 coproduct.\",\n        \"If you intend the Bada et al. branch, use the already balanced threonine -> glycine + ethylene oxide representation.\",\n        \"If you intend the Vallentyne branch, introduce the experimentally justified coproduct rather than leaving it implicit.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P9\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_009\": 0.92\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.92,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.88,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.91,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.95,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.91,\n      \"feedback\": \"This is a strong pathway. rxn_009 is balanced, chemically plausible, hydrothermally consistent, and directly aligned with Bada et al. 1978 for threonine decomposition to glycine plus ethylene oxide.\",\n      \"approved\": true,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Add explicit hydrothermal temperature information to match the cited experiments.\",\n        \"Optionally clarify whether ethylene oxid