oning\": \"Muchowska et al. (2019) experimentally established that glyoxylate is a direct hydrothermal precursor to glycine, but their system used hydroxylamine and iron. Because the review flags hydroxylamine availability as an open question, and because explicit redox closure is needed in a node-based network, this step uses a stoichiometrically closed NH3 and H2 vent analogue on iron sulfide surfaces. This specific net reaction is speculative relative to the supplied literature, but it preserves the same geochemical logic that glyoxylate is the immediate C2 precursor of glycine under H2-rich hydrothermal conditions (Muchowska et al., 2019).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_004\",\n      \"name\": \"UV dehydration of 2,2-dihydroxyacetic acid to glyoxylate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_009\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"ultraviolet radiation\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"UV-irradiated shallow pool under anoxic atmosphere\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Photochemical dehydration of the gem-diol regenerates the aldehydic oxoacid glyoxylate.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Liu et al. (2021) directly support UV dehydration of 2,2-dihydroxyacetic acid, the hydrate of glyoxylic acid, to glyoxylate in irradiated surface waters. This is a well supported photochemical entry into the glyoxylate branch of the glycine network.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_005\",\n      \"name\": \"Peracetic acid amination to glycine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_001\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"350-430K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"high\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        