 et al., 2019)."}, {"id": "rxn_004", "name": "UV dehydration of 2,2-dihydroxyacetic acid to glyoxylate", "inputs": ["mol_009"], "outputs": ["mol_007", "mol_002"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["ultraviolet radiation"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "UV-irradiated shallow pool under anoxic atmosphere"}, "mechanism": "Photochemical dehydration of the gem-diol regenerates the aldehydic oxoacid glyoxylate.", "reasoning": "Liu et al. (2021) directly support UV dehydration of 2,2-dihydroxyacetic acid, the hydrate of glyoxylic acid, to glyoxylate in irradiated surface waters. This is a well supported photochemical entry into the glyoxylate branch of the glycine network."}, {"id": "rxn_005", "name": "Peracetic acid amination to glycine", "inputs": ["mol_010", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_001", "mol_002"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": [], "conditions": {"temperature": "350-430K", "pressure": "high", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Submarine hydrothermal setting with reduced ocean chemistry and no O2"}, "mechanism": "Ammonia attacks the peroxy-activated acetyl carbon, giving C-N bond formation and net conversion to glycine with water as coproduct.", "reasoning": "Aubrey, Cleaves, and Bada (2008) reported glycine formation from peracetic acid and NH3 under submarine hydrothermal conditions. The explicit H2O coproduct closes the oxygen and hydrogen balance. The main caveat, also noted in the review, is that prebiotic peracetic acid availability remains uncertain."}, {"id": "rxn_006", "name": "Serine thermal decomposition to glycine and formaldehyde", "inputs": ["mol_011"], "outputs": ["mol_001", "mol_004"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": [], "conditions": {"temperature": "380-470K", "pressure": "high", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Sustained hydrothermal heating"}, "mechanism": "Retro-aldol cleavage breaks the C-C bond between the alpha carbon and the hydroxymethyl side chain, releasing formaldehyde.", "reasoning": "Bada et al. (1978) demonstrated thermal decomposition of serine to glycine plus formaldehyde. This is best interpreted as hydrothermal recycling rather than de novo C2 construction and supports the view that glycine is a thermodynamic sink for more complex amino acids under prolonged heating."}, {"id": "rxn_007", "name": "Threonine thermal decomposition to glycine and acetaldehyde", "inputs": ["mol_012"], "outputs": ["mol_001", "mol_014"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": [], "conditions": {"temperature": "380-470K", "pressure": "high", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic hydrothermal heating; mass-balanced encoding of the C2 side-chain loss route"}, "mechanism": "Thermal fragmentation removes the C2 side chain as acetaldehyde while retaining the glycine backbone.", "reasoning": "Vallentyne (1964) showed threonine degrades thermally to glycine, and the review summarizes the missing C2 fragment in that simpler route as acetaldehyde or an equivalent fragment. Acetaldehyde is used here as the explicit mass-balanced coproduct. The precise C2 coproduct is less directly constrained than in the ethylene oxide route, so this node is literature-guided but mechanistically less secure than Bada et al. (1978)."}, {"id": "rxn_008", "name": "Threonine thermal decomposition to glycine and ethylene oxide", "inputs": ["mol_012"], "outputs": ["mol_001", "mol_013"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": [], "conditions": {"temperature": "380-470K", "pressure": "high", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic hydrothermal heating with retro-aldol-type side-chain loss"}, "mechanism": "Retro-aldol-type cleavage is followed by intramolecular cyclization of the side-chain fragment to ethylene oxide.", "reasoning": "Bada et al. (1978) provided the more explicit mechanistic variant in which threonine yields glycine plus ethylene oxide under hydrothermal heating. This mass-balanced formulation is directly supported in the supplied review and complements Vallentyne's broader observation that glycine is the dominant stable product."}, {"id": "rxn_009", "name": "Asparagine hydrolytic decomposition to glycine", "inputs": ["mol_015", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_001"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": [], "conditions": {"temperature": "420-520K", "pressure": "high", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Deep hydrothermal hydrolysis; net stoichiometry gives two glycine equivalents"}, "mechanism": "Side-chain amide hydrolysis is followed by high-temperature backbone fragmentation to glycine units.", "reasoning": "Qian et al. (1993) reported high-temperature hydrolytic decomposition of asparagine to two glycine equivalents. The reaction node therefore lists asparagine and water as inputs and glycine as the sole product, with the second glycine coefficient inferred automatically from mass balance."}], "pathways": [{"pathway_id": "P1", "name": "Direct aminonitrile hydrolysis", "description": "Single-step surface hydrolysis of preformed aminoacetonitrile to glycine.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_002"], "key_intermediates": []}, {"pathway_id": "P2", "name": "Surface Strecker route", "description": "Formaldehyde, HCN, and NH3 form aminoacetonitrile in a cyanosulfidic pool, followed by hydrolysis to glycine.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_006"]}, {"pathway_id": "P3", "name": "Direct glyoxylate amination", "description": "Hydrothermal conversion of glyoxylate to glycine in H2-rich vent water by the stoichiometrically explicit NH3 and H2 analogue of the iron-promoted glyoxylate route.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_003"], "key_intermediates": []}, {"pathway_id": "P4", "name": "UV glyoxylate-hydrate route", "description": "Surface UV dehydration converts glyoxylic acid hydrate to glyoxylate, which then enters hydrothermal amination chemistry to yield glycine.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_004", "rxn_003"], "k