nversion to glycine. The surface-to-hydrothermal handoff of glyoxylate is plausible from the supplied environmental discussion, but it is only implicit and should be stated more clearly.", "approved": false, "problematic_reactions": ["rxn_003"], "suggestions": ["Replace or strengthen rxn_003 with an experimentally supported glyoxylate amination step.", "Add an explicit transport rationale for glyoxylate between UV surface pools and hydrothermal settings, or keep both steps in one justified environment."]}, {"pathway_id": "P5", "reaction_scores": {"rxn_005": 0.89}, "chemical_feasibility": 0.88, "pathway_coherence": 0.93, "environmental_progression": 0.9, "target_achievement": 1.0, "overall_confidence": 0.89, "feedback": "A direct hydrothermal route with good literature support. The balanced form written here, with H2O as coproduct, is chemically cleaner than the simplified shorthand often used in summaries. This is a credible one-step path to glycine under the stated environment.", "approved": true, "problematic_reactions": [], "suggestions": ["State the elevated hydrothermal temperature or reaction window explicitly.", "If you extend the network upstream, add a justified source of peracetic acid to improve pathway closure."]}, {"pathway_id": "P6", "reaction_scores": {"rxn_006": 0.95}, "chemical_feasibility": 0.95, "pathway_coherence": 0.95, "environmental_progression": 0.94, "target_achievement": 1.0, "overall_confidence": 0.95, "feedback": "This is the most robust pathway in the network. rxn_006 is balanced, mechanistically plausible as retro-aldol cleavage, environmentally consistent with hydrothermal decomposition studies, and directly supported by the supplied literature.", "approved": true, "problematic_reactions": [], "suggestions": ["Retain this as a high-confidence hydrothermal recycling module.", "If desired, annotate approximate temperature range or residence time to strengthen environmental specificity."]}, {"pathway_id": "P7", "reaction_scores": {"rxn_00