"mol_012\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H4O3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"peroxyacetic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"peracetic acid\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C2H4O3/c1-2(3)5-4/h4H,1H3\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_013\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H7NO3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-amino-3-hydroxypropanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"L-serine\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C3H7NO3/c4-2(1-5)3(6)7/h2,5H,1,4H2,(H,6,7)\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_014\",\n      \"formula\": \"C6H8O7\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"1-hydroxypropane-1,2,3-tricarboxylic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"isocitric acid\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C6H8O7/c7-3(8)1-2(5(10)11)4(9)6(12)13/h2,4,9H,1H2,(H,7,8)(H,10,11)(H,12,13)\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_015\",\n      \"formula\": \"C4H6O4\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"butanedioic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"succinate\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C4H6O4/c5-3(6)1-2-4(7)8/h1-2H2,(H,5,6)(H,7,8)\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Hydrothermal\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"reactions\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_001\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyanosulfidic Strecker formation of aminoacetonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_003\", \"mol_004\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"anoxic HCN-rich evaporitic or tidal-flat pool; concentration during drying and reaction during rewetting\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Ammonia first forms an imine/iminium equivalent with formaldehyde, then cyanide adds to give the aminonitrile; the net condensation releases water.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Patel et al. (2015) place aminoacetonitrile in the cyanosulfidic network as the Strecker aminonitrile precursor to glycine. Writing water explicitly corrects the bookkeeping problem noted in earlier failed attempts. The route is experimentally well supported, although aminonitrile stability under intense UV and fluctuating pH remains a recognized limitation (Patel et al., 2015).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_002\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrolysis of aminoacetonitrile to glycine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_001\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"wet phase after cyanosulfidic concentration; neutral to mildly alkaline water\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"The nitrile hydrates stepwise through an amide-stage equivalent and then to the carboxylic acid, releasing ammonia.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is the direct glycine-forming step from the Patel et al. (2015) cyanosulfidic framework. With inferred stoichiometry the encoded species set corresponds to aminoacetonitrile + 2 H2O -> glycine + NH3, which fixes the under-specified hydrolysis from prior attempts. Side reactions and the persistence of aminoacetonitrile remain open issues, but the step itself is experimentally grounded (Patel et al., 2015).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_003\",\n      \"name\": \"UV dehydration of glyoxylic acid hydrate to glyoxylate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_011\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"UV radiation\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"UV radiation\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"UV-irradiated shallow pool under anoxic photoredox conditions\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Photochemical loss of water from the gem-diol regenerates the aldehydic glyoxylate form.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Liu et al. (2021) showed UV photoredox access to glyoxylate from 2,2-dihydroxyacetic acid/glyoxylic acid hydrate. Water is written explicitly so the dehydration is balanced. This is a strong surface photochemical entry point into the glyoxylate-glycine network (Liu et al., 2021).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_004\",\n      \"name\": \"Fe-assisted reductive amination of glyoxylate with hydroxylamine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_008\", \"mol_009\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_001\", \"mol_010\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"elemental iron (Fe0)\", \"ferrous iron (Fe2+)\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"340-350K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"moderate-high\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"ferrous iron (Fe2+); Fe0 supplies reducing equivalents\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"anoxic alkaline hydrothermal fluid or vent mixing zone; ferrous hydroxide used as explicit iron oxidation sink\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydroxylamine donates nitrogen to glyoxylate through an N-O-containing intermediate, and Fe0/Fe2+ drive reduction to the amino acid.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Muchowska, Varma, and Moran (2019) experimentally demonstrated glycine formation from glyoxylate and hydroxylamine with Fe0/Fe2+ at 70 C. The literature shorthand omits explicit redox products; the balanced net encoded here corresponds to glyoxylate + hydroxylamine + 2 Fe0 + 2 H2O -> glycine + 2 Fe(OH)2, using ferrous hydroxide as a plausible anoxic alkaline sink for oxidized iron. This keeps the experimentally supported hydroxylamine donor and addresses the bookkeeping problem that undermined earlier attempts. Hydroxylamine availability and exact iron mineralogy remain open questions (Muchowska et al., 2019).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_005\",\n      \"name\": \"Fe2+-catalyzed cleavage of isocitrate to glyoxylate and succinate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_014\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_015\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"ferrous iron (Fe2+)\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"340-350K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"moderate-high\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"ferrous iron (Fe2+)\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"anoxic hydrothermal water; metabolic-analog network chemistry\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Fe2+ acts as a Lewis acid and promotes retro-aldol cleavage of the C6 tricarboxylate into a C2 glyoxylate fragment and a C4 succinate fragment.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Muchowska et al. (2019) reported Fe2+-promoted cleavage of isocitrate to glyoxylate plus succinate under mild hydrothermal conditions, providing a prebiotic analog of isocitrate lyase. This gives a second experimentally supported entry into the glyoxylate hub without adding artificial connectivity (Muchowska et al., 2019).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_006\",\n      \"name\": \"Amination of peracetic acid by ammonia to glycine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_001\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"350-400K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"moderate-high\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"submarine hydrothermal setting; anoxic\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Ammonia attacks the activated peroxyacetyl carbonyl, and collapse of the peroxide-derived intermediate gives glycine in the net stoichiometry.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Aubrey, Cleaves, and Bada (2008) reported glycine formation from peracetic acid and ammonia under submarine hydrothermal conditions. Including water explicitly gives a balanced net reaction. The major caveat is the uncertain prebiotic supply of peracetic acid itself, which the review identifies as unresolved (Aubrey et al., 2008).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_007\",\n      \"name\": \"Thermal decomposition of serine to glycine and formaldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_013\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_001\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"370-450K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"moderate-high\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"prolonged hydrothermal heating; anoxic\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Retro-aldol cleavage of the Calpha-Cbeta bond releases the hydroxymethyl side chain as formaldehyde and leaves the glycine backbone.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Bada, Shou, Man, and Schroeder (1978) directly demonstrated serine -> glycine + formaldehyde under hydrothermal heating. Writing formaldehyde explicitly preserves mass balance and reveals a chemically meaningful bridge from hydrothermal amino-acid degradation into the surface aminonitrile module (Bada et al., 1978).\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"Surface Strecker-hydrolysis route\",\n      \"description\": \"Formaldehyde, HCN, and ammonia in a cyanosulfidic surface pool form aminoacetonitrile, which hydrolyzes during a wet phase to glycine.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_006\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P2\",\n      \"name\": \"Direct aminonitrile hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"If aminoacetonitrile is already available, simple aqueous hydrolysis gives glycine directly.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_002\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P3\",\n      \"name\": \"UV glyoxylate-hydrate route\",\n      \"description\": \"UV dehydration of glyoxylic acid hydrate generates glyoxylate, which is then converted to glycine by Fe-assisted reductive amination.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_007\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P4\",\n      \"name\": \"Direct hydrothermal glyoxylate amination\",\n      \"description\": \"Glyoxylate present in Fe-rich hydrothermal water is directly converted to glycine with hydroxylamine.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_004\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P5\",\n      \"name\": \"Isocitrate cleavage route\",\n      \"description\": \"Hydrothermal Fe2+-catalyzed cleavage of isocitrate produces glyoxylate, which then feeds the Fe-assisted amination step to glycine.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_005\", \"rxn_004\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_007\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P6\",\n      \"name\": \"Peracetic acid amination route\",\n      \"description\": \"Peracetic acid reacts with ammonia under hydrothermal conditions to give glycine directly.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_006\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P7\",\n      \"name\": \"Serine thermolysis route\",\n      \"description\": \"Hydrothermal retro-aldol decomposition of serine yields glycine and formaldehyde.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_007\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P8\",\n      \"name\": \"Serine-to-aminonitrile relay\",\n      \"description\": \"Serine thermolysis liberates formaldehyde; after transport into a cyanosulfidic surface pool it can be trapped as aminoacetonitrile and hydrolyzed to an additional glycine equivalent.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_007\", \"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_003\", \"mol_006\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P9\",\n      \"name\": \"Strecker-peracid tandem route\",\n      \"description\": \"Surface Strecker chemistry yields aminoacetonitrile and then glycine; the ammonia released on hydrolysis can be recaptured by hydrothermal peracetic acid to generate a second glycine equivalent.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_006\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_005\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P10\",\n      \"name\": \"Serine-driven multi-module glycine amplification\",\n      \"description\": \"A hydrothermal-surface-hydrothermal relay: serine decomposition supplies formaldehyde for Strecker aminonitrile formation, and the hydrolysis-released ammonia is then reused in peracetic acid amination, giving three glycine equivalents overall.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_007\", \"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_006\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_003\", \"mol_006\", \"mol_005\"]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"hub_molecules\": [\"mol_001\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_003\", \"mol_005\", \"mol_006\", \"mol_007\"],\n  \"convergence_points\": [\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_003\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_007\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_001\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_005\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_002\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_006\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_006\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_001\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_002\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_007\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_003\", \"rxn_005\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_004\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_002\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_006\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_004\"]\n    }\n  ]\n}", "parsed_response": {"target_molecule": "Glycine", "overall_strategy": "The network combines the strongest experimentally supported glycine-forming modules while keeping every encoded step atom-balanced: a surface cyanosulfidic Strecker/aminonitrile branch, a hydrothermal glyoxylate branch fed by either UV dehydration of glyoxylic acid hydrate or Fe2+-mediated isocitrate cleavage, a direct hydrothermal peracetic-acid amination route, and hydrothermal serine thermolysis. To avoid the under-justified shortcut criticized in earlier attempts, the glyoxylate-to-glycine step retains the documented hydroxylamine plus Fe0/Fe2+ chemistry and closes redox bookkeeping explicitly with an iron oxidation product. Ten pathways arise because formaldehyde and ammonia naturally relay between these modules.", "molecules": [{"id": "mol_001", "formula": "C2H5NO2", "iupac_name": "aminoacetic acid", "common_name": "glycine", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C2H5NO2/c3-1-2(4)5/h1,3H2,(H,4,5)", "role": "target", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_002", "formula": "H2O", "iupac_name": "oxidane", "common_name": "water", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2O/h1H2", "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_003", "formula": "CH2O", "iupac_name": "methanal", "common_name": "formaldehyde", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CH2O/c1-2/h1H2", "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_004", "formula": "HCN", "iupac_name": "hydrogen cyanide", "common_name": "hydrogen cyanide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CHN/c1-2/h1H", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_005", "formula": "NH3", "iupac_name": "azane", "common_name": "ammonia", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3", "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_006", "formula": "C2H4N2", "iupac_name": "2-aminoethanenitrile", "common_name": "aminoacetonitrile", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C2H4N2/c3-1-2-4/h1,3H2", "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_007", "formula": "C2H2O3", "iupac_name": "2-oxoacetic acid", "common_name": "glyoxylic acid", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C2H2O3/c3-1-2(4)5/h1H,(