environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["ferrous iron (Fe2+)"], "conditions": {"temperature": "340-350K", "pressure": "moderate-high", "catalyst": "ferrous iron (Fe2+)", "aqueous": true, "other": "anoxic hydrothermal water; metabolic-analog network chemistry"}, "mechanism": "Fe2+ acts as a Lewis acid and promotes retro-aldol cleavage of the C6 tricarboxylate into a C2 glyoxylate fragment and a C4 succinate fragment.", "reasoning": "Muchowska et al. (2019) reported Fe2+-promoted cleavage of isocitrate to glyoxylate plus succinate under mild hydrothermal conditions, providing a prebiotic analog of isocitrate lyase. This gives a second experimentally supported entry into the glyoxylate hub without adding artificial connectivity (Muchowska et al., 2019)."}, {"id": "rxn_006", "name": "Amination of peracetic acid by ammonia to glycine", "inputs": ["mol_012", "mol_005"], "outputs": ["mol_001", "mol_002"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "350-400K", "pressure": "moderate-high", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "submarine hydrothermal setting; anoxic"}, "mechanism": "Ammonia attacks the activated peroxyacetyl carbonyl, and collapse of the peroxide-derived intermediate gives glycine in the net stoichiometry.", "reasoning": "Aubrey, Cleaves, and Bada (2008) reported glycine formation from peracetic acid and ammonia under submarine hydrothermal conditions. Including water explicitly gives a balanced net reaction. The major caveat is the uncertain prebiotic supply of peracetic acid itself, which the review identifies as unresolved (Aubrey et al., 2008)."}, {"id": "rxn_007", "name": "Thermal decomposition of serine to glycine and formaldehyde", "inputs": ["mol_013"], "outputs": ["mol_001", "mol_003"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "370-450K", "pressure": "moderate-high", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "prolonged hydrothermal heating; anoxic"}, "mechanism": "Retro-a